ly see this  co-expression  of protein a's mrna and cd68 mrna  i'm going to zoom in so that you can see  that  and i have an arrow showing a macrophage  with protein a  mrna so what was very interesting about  this result is that we found that  only the macrophages found near the  lumen of the colonic tissue were  expressing protein a  mrna so this was truly a new discovery  um not all the macrophages were  expressing protein a as we thought  before and it really was a subset of  macrophages that  were able to visualize for the first  time  looking at the tissue architecture  so my next example is  [Music]  an example of using two plex in feature  hybridization to detect two rna species  when we looked when we were looking for  a subset of cd4 positive t  cells uh fox b3 positive cells that are  expressing a specific  chemokine receptor so a little  background on the study  the specific t cells comprise a really  low percentage of the population i think  we're looking  at about three to five percent of the  total cd4 positive cells found in human  tissues  and we were expecting an increase when  looking at diseased tissues and we  wanted to be able to detect this  ihc regions were available to test we  actually did have a nice robust  ihc assay we but we wanted to know if it  was real staining  because if we're looking at a three to  five percent population of cd4 positive  t  cells we wanted to be sure that we were  looking um  or or that we were getting the results  that were true and specific  so in this case we wanted to  identify that the robust ic reagent was  truly specific  and that it will also detect a chemokine  which we're going to call chemokine r  expressed in a subset of fox p3 positive  t  cells so here i have head and neck  cancer  and you see fox p3 ihc on the right  panel  and what we wanted to do was in that  population  determine if any of those were fox p3  and chemokine are positive so again  we're looking at a population that's  comprised of 