8  positive t  cells and then where you see red is  a dual expression of cd8 and the  chemokine receptor  so we were able to examine if this  specific population of cells  were affecting chronically infected  chimpanzees  with hpv and so we were able to apply  this onto the  chimp tissues  so i want to get into other everyday  applications  that we like to use in cg hybridization  just standard fish assays no dual assays  or anything like that and we basically  use them  almost on a weekly basis to validate  antibodies  validate our reagents and we also use  them to identify proteins that either  have poor retention in ffpe  or where there's no antibody available  to use for  ihd purposes so i want to  talk about protein x protein x is a  secreted matrix enzyme  um and we knew it was highly expressed  in colorectal carcinomas  and so typically when you do ihc work  you work with  ffpe blocks and so on the left  we have protein x ihc  and if one were to look at this you  would think well it's not that highly  expressed in this colorectal carcinoma  tissue  it kind of looks like a murky stain  maybe even non-specific staining  but this puzzled us because we knew that  protein x was highly expressed  in colorectal carcinoma so what  we did was we looked at the fresh frozen  component of this tissue  this is not a cereal section but i will  show you a few in the next couple slides  um then we got this result on the right  where we stained  protein x antibody on fresh frozen  colorectal carcinoma tissue a very very  different  staining pattern that you see here we  see a lot of staining in the stroma  and no staining in the tumor cells and  we wanted to know is this  specific is this correct and so the way  we were able to answer that question  is we if you look on the left there's  ihc  on fresh rails and tissues and on the  right we did in situ hybridization  on the fresh frozen tissues the s stands  for stroma  so just as we saw these are cereal  sections we were able to detect the mrna  of protein x in the stroma and little to  none  on the tumor cells i think i have a  zoomed in image here showing that these  were actually  activated fibroblasts that were making  um protein x and it was actually  secreted  into the stromal component of the tissue  whereas this tumor the tumor cells are  almost completely negative  here's the example i showed two slides  ago on the left was the ihc  and on the right is in situ  hybridization so  again we were able to detect the stromal  cells  these are activated fibroblasts that's  where the arrows pointing to  and in this case we saw that some tumor  cells were slightly positive so  that blushy stain that you see on the  left with the ffpe  sample those are that that really was a  true stain it wasn't stickiness  but we couldn't confirm it until we saw  the mrna  being detected uh by by using in c2  hybridization  so this is a close-up again showing the  activated fibroblasts within that narrow  stroma  the tumor cells close to that lumen  where the l is  those are slightly positive so what you  see on the left with ffpe  is a true signal and we knew that we  were able to that we were actually  detecting  protein x by by confirming through in c2  hybridization  i like to show this picture because one  thing about protein x that  we know is that um it's  secreted produced and secreted by  activated fibroblasts  and these fibroblasts are activated more  closely to the tumor  stromal boundaries so i have that  depicted there by  t colon s we see the tumor cells  with the t and then we see the ts tumor  stromal boundary  and if you were to look at an ffp image  which is on the left  with ihc it again looks like a very  sticky blushy stain  but when we did in situ hybridization we  were able to confirm that  that is true expression of protein x  at that tumor stromal boundary  so lastly i like to show this example as  well  where we had a really nice working  uh robust ihc assay for protein z  and