o  knockout cell pellets  we work with a lot of phosphatase  specific antibodies  and so we're able to treat with  phosphatase treatment to verify target  fossil specificity with cell pellets  so these are definitely reagents that we  like to use and have  when we do our antibody validation  so the way we go about it and this is  this was for a prostate target  we'll call it prostate target x we make  a cell micro  array we call them cmas not really tmas  they're not tissues so we call them cmas  cell microarrays  and here we have an example of 10  different  cell types um they all range in in  either being negative  um expression for for prostate target x  or a medium expression or high  and here it was nice when we worked with  the scientists we were able to have  an orthogonal method to compare uh when  we validate our reagents this is  facts analysis of protein x and  um you can see that there are actually  three cell types that are positive  for protein x and then you have six  other cell types that are negative  and the positivity did range from low  medium and high so it's really nice to  get that range of expression  in order to do validation of your of  your reagents  so when we tested several antibodies we  found the best one was this monoclonal  antibody  that seemed to adequate adequately label  the cell pellets  you can see the facts data in the bottom  right hand corner of each panel  but the three positive cell types or  cell lines  that were deemed positive by facts also  checked out through ihc  um this is this is a pretty clear  um go or no-go decision but since then  we've always incorporated in c2  hybridization  so we went ahead and did the mrna  expression analysis  by fish on these cell pellets and  what was comforting and pleasing to see  was that the fish data corroborated with  the ihc data that was produced  which also corroborated with the facts  data  so in summary um i hope i was able to  convince you that  using chromogenic and c2 hybridization  is a  powerful technique to localize  mrna within fixed or fresh frozen  tissues one thing i didn't mention but  it's worth mentioning now is  when you use chromogenic and feature  hybridization and not fluorescence  although fluorophores are much more  stable nowadays  all you need for fish is light  microscopy so these flies are archivable  you never lose their signal and i've  stained slides from seven ten years ago  and they're still bright with the  chromogenic and feature hybridization  signals  using fish or dual fish or dual  ihc and fish it really allows you to  determine  exact cell types involved not only does  it help you  identify cell types but also subtypes of  specific cells  we've used them as an adjunct to ic  assays confirming ambiguous ihc results  or also confirming expression when we  don't have  a good iec reagent the data can be  generated from both fresh frozen or ffpe  tissues where i showed you  and in the example where we had that  secreted  enzyme within the matrix the ihc  only worked in fresh frozen and not in  ffpe so we were able to  make sure that that was a true signal  and we use this technique to validate  reagents for ihc  method development now it's kind of like  one of the steps that we  um it's one of the check marks that we  have to check when we do antibody  validation  and so using all these methods have  provided valuable  and new insights to many programs at  gilead  and so um it's always good to have  orthogonal methods  but um just being able to  incorporate different assays to  determine specific  specificity is our number one goal  within the lab  so i'd like to acknowledge um the folks  in the non-clinical safety and  pathobiology group  chris clark and laurie deal who are my  managers and rosanna  and alison who are instrumental in  getting a lot of these assays up and  running  amanda nichols vigdell was involved with  a lot of the studies  that i discussed today and there are  many folks at  acd advanc